fact WorkItem(id: "WI-235", description: some(value: "Operation-call-model: let-hoist for repeated construct_requirement literals. WI-234's Pin-now path emits apply_within(..., requirements=[construct_requirement(impl, [...])]) per call site. Hash-consing dedupes the TermId across structurally-identical inputs, but each runtime evaluation of construct_requirement allocates a fresh arena slot. A body with multiple Pin-now calls sharing the same dispatching dict (e.g. `if eq(1,2) then eq(3,4) else eq(5,6)` at T=Int) pays N allocations for one logically-shared dict. Scope: extend req_insertion::run (kb/req_insertion.rs) — no separate pass needed. The hoist becomes a phase of the existing pass: (a) group classifications by owning op-body (CallClass gains an enclosing_op_body_root: TermId field, populated by the typer at classify time); (b) per-body, scan the group to identify construct_requirement shapes that will be emitted more than once; (c) assign fresh synthesized names (e.g. __hoisted_req_<n>) for each duplicated shape; (d) emit per-call rewrites using var_ref(name) at the duplicate sites and inline construct_requirement at singletons; (e) emit a body-root rewrite that wraps the original body in let_expr bindings for each hoisted shape; (f) extend eval_requirement_chain (eval/eval.rs) to handle var_ref: resolve the name in frame.locals, expect Value::Requirement(handle), return handle.clone() (clear error otherwise). Storage: frame.locals — existing channel for let-bound values; no migration of frame.requirements needed. Concerns: (a) scoping — don't shadow existing user-level let-patterns, pick fresh symbols; (b) cross-branch placement — a construct_requirement appearing in both if/match branches hoists to body-root only when safe (in v0 construct_requirement bodies are Symbol literals + nested construct_requirements with no free locals, so this is trivially safe); (c) lambda capture — closures over hoisted var_refs capture the binding from the enclosing frame via the existing closure mechanism; (d) eligibility — hoist only when the duplicated subterm has no requirement_at_current chain inside it (those depend on frame context); pure-static construct_requirement(impl, nil) and nested chains thereof are always eligible. Out of scope: cross-body sharing (would need const_requirement + runtime cache — separate WI if motivated by profiling); frame.requirements rekey-to-symbol-keyed migration (separate, larger WI from WI-234's deferred roadmap). Acceptance: cargo-test green; new tests pin (1) a body with three identical Pin-now eq calls at T=Int rewrites to a body-root let-wrapping with one construct_requirement and three var_refs; (2) rewritten body produces the same Value as the un-hoisted form; (3) requirement arena live-count delta after running the body equals the number of hoisted bindings, not the call count. References: WI-234 (Model 1 — supersedes WI-229's projection-hoist scope with this construct_requirement-hoist scope); stale WI-229 for design-concern inheritance; docs/design/operation-call-model.md §'Construction site'."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-234"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-05-13T01:50:00Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-235", author: "user", content: "Superseded by WI-237 Phase 2 (commit 333d4cf, 2026-05-15). The let-hoist for duplicate construct_requirement shapes was removed when WI-237 migrated to the names model: under names model, the dispatching dict is constructed once at the call site (via apply_within's requirements channel) and held name-keyed in frame.requirements[__req_<spec>], so body sites do var_ref(__req_<spec>) → frame lookup without allocation. The old optimization (let-bind a duplicated body-level construct_requirement once at body root, reference via var_ref(__hoist_N)) was rendered obsolete because there is no longer a body-level construct_requirement to dedupe. Dormant infrastructure left by the removal: kb.op_body_overrides: HashMap<Symbol, OccurrenceId> (eval::lookup_operation_body still consults it, no producer remains), and kb.set_op_body_override / op_body_override accessors. WI-243 (2026-05-15) migrated op_body_overrides's value type from TermId to OccurrenceId for forward-compat with the OccurrenceOrigin substrate. The infrastructure is forward-ready for any future pass that wants to install body-level let-wraps (constraint elaboration, monomorphization).", at: "2026-05-15T12:24:44Z")
